# The address the OTA response hands the device. Left unset, the bridge picks a
# LAN address itself; set it when that pick is wrong (a second adapter, a VPN).
#BRIDGE_PUBLIC_HOST=192.168.1.100

# Where the two servers listen. These are the ports the flashed device expects.
#OTA_PORT=8003
#WS_PORT=8000

# The secret every device's token is derived from. REQUIRED, at least 32
# characters, and never reused between deployments: whoever holds it can connect
# as any device on the allowlist. It is the one secret this service keeps off
# hardware. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#BRIDGE_DEVICE_SECRET=

# Where adaptive-interview-api is, as a base URL. REQUIRED, and with no default,
# unlike the ports above: those are addresses this machine serves on, but this
# one belongs to a deployment, and the only obvious default is localhost — right
# on one machine and silently wrong on every other, where the bridge would come up
# looking healthy and reach nothing. Any trailing slash is dropped when this is
# read, so leave it off or leave it on, whichever you prefer.
#BRIDGE_PLATFORM_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000

# The platform's own credential, as the platform has it in its .env. REQUIRED.
# This is the value the bridge calls adaptive-interview-api with, server to
# server, on a device's behalf — and the one credential a device must never hold:
# a gadget authenticates to this bridge with the token the OTA endpoint issues it,
# and with nothing else. Copy it from adaptive-interview-api's .env, and never
# onto hardware. There is no length rule: it is the platform's token, carried
# unchanged, and the platform compares it byte for byte.
#API_AUTH_TOKEN=

# The devices this bridge will provision, as their Device-Id (a MAC address).
# Separators and case do not matter, so b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01 and B8-1F-3F-4A-9B-01
# name the same device. EMPTY MEANS NOBODY: a device is allowed because it is
# named here, and every other device is refused at the OTA endpoint and at the
# socket.
#BRIDGE_ALLOWED_DEVICES=b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01,3c:0f:02:d0:7e:fc

# Which persona each device speaks as, as DEVICE=PERSONA, one per device named
# above. Separators and case are read the same way the allowlist reads them, so
# the lists can be copied from the same place. All three lists below must describe
# the same devices in both directions: a device that may connect and has no
# persona could never be answered, one with no language or engine could never be
# heard or spoken to, and a binding for a device the allowlist does not name is
# configuration for something that can never connect. Any of them is refused at
# start, naming the device and the kind of binding it is missing. The persona
# identifier is the catalog's own id (GET /api/personas) — the bridge holds no
# personas of its own, and a gadget speaks as exactly one.
#BRIDGE_DEVICE_PERSONAS=b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01=language-partner,3c:0f:02:d0:7e:fc=friendly-tutor

# The language each device is heard and answered in, as DEVICE=english|vietnamese.
# Declared on every turn that device takes, because the platform decodes the
# utterance in the language the turn names rather than detecting one, and reads an
# absent value as English: a Vietnamese gadget whose turns omitted this would come
# back in a foreign script while the bridge looked healthy. It is also the language
# the device's own spoken commands are matched against, so a gadget hears the
# commands of the language it is bound to and no other.
#BRIDGE_DEVICE_LANGUAGES=b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01=vietnamese,3c:0f:02:d0:7e:fc=english

# The synthesis engine each device is spoken through, as
# DEVICE=kokoro|piper|supertonic. Declared on every turn that device takes, because
# an absent engine is NOT "no preference": the platform folds an unknown or missing
# one to `kokoro` *before* it consults its own default, so a turn that dropped this
# would ask for an engine this deployment may never have chosen. Anything outside
# the three is refused at start rather than folded, so a typo is an error rather
# than a silent change of voice.
#
# This is only half of how a device sounds. The *voice* is the platform's, resolved
# from this engine and this language together — `resolveVoice(engine, language)` in
# adaptive-interview-api — out of that repository's own SUPERTONIC_VOICE_*,
# PIPER_VOICE_* and KOKORO_VOICE_* settings. The bridge names no voice, because a
# voice it could name would be a second place for one decision to live.
#
# So the two are one decision per device, and not every pair can be spoken: the
# engines hold different models, and binding a device to an engine with no voice
# configured for its language is a turn the platform cannot synthesize. It fails
# there rather than here, loudly, naming the voices it does hold — so set these and
# check that repository's voices together, per device. Kokoro holds a Vietnamese
# model only on this deployment; piper and supertonic hold both languages.
#BRIDGE_DEVICE_ENGINES=b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01=piper,3c:0f:02:d0:7e:fc=kokoro

# Whether this deployment's two gadgets hold a conversation, as one DEVICE=DEVICE pair. OFF
# UNLESS SET, and at most one pair: a bridge that records none reaches none of the work this
# setting turns on and behaves exactly as it did before the setting existed.
#
# What it does. When a turn on one gadget has been answered, the bridge asks its partner for a
# turn whose input is that reply's OWN WORDS — as text, and not a transcription of what the
# partner's microphone heard — and holds the answer until the gadget that was speaking reports
# that it has begun listening (its own `listen start`, 0.04-0.26s after its speaker stops,
# measured 2026-10-17). The two therefore take turns, and the answer is already produced by the
# time the other stops speaking, so the silence between them is BRIDGE_HANDOVER_MS and not the
# platform's own latency.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, and this is the whole of it: while an exchange is running, neither gadget's
# microphone is judged — a lull inside the other gadget's reply cannot close a turn — so a
# person is heard only through the wake word. The wake word is also what ends an exchange: it
# cancels the turn in flight and drops the reply that was prepared. An exchange with nobody in
# the room ends on BRIDGE_SESSION_IDLE_MS.
#
# Both devices must be in BRIDGE_ALLOWED_DEVICES, and a pair naming one that is not is refused
# at start like every other malformed list.
#BRIDGE_DEVICE_PARTNERS=b8:1f:3f:4a:9b:01=3c:0f:02:d0:7e:fc

# How long after the floor is free the partner's turn is spoken, in ms. Read from
# BRIDGE_HANDOVER_MS; four tenths of a second when unset.
#
# A feel rather than a measurement, like BRIDGE_COMMAND_STEP and BRIDGE_MUSIC_GAIN_DB: a tenth
# of a second reads as a machine cutting in, and four tenths is the top of a natural gap between
# two people. It is also the WHOLE of the silence a person hears between the two gadgets, since
# the answer is prepared while the other is still speaking — so this one figure is the pace of
# the conversation, and whether it wants to be shorter is settled by listening.
#BRIDGE_HANDOVER_MS=400

# The binary framing version. The firmware negotiates one of 3, 2, 1.
#BRIDGE_FRAMING=3

# What the server hello declares it will send. 24000 is the platform's measured
# Kokoro output rate and a legal Opus rate; the firmware defaults to the same, so
# nothing resamples.
#BRIDGE_SERVER_RATE=24000
#BRIDGE_FRAME_MS=60

# THE ENDPOINTER — how the bridge decides the person has stopped speaking. The
# firmware sends no `listen stop`, so nothing else says (design.md D9), and the
# defaults below are all measurements rather than preferences. Leave them unset
# unless a deployment's room is unusual enough to justify a figure of your own.

# The gate: the quietest frame that counts at all, in RMS, and how far above the
# room's own estimated floor a frame must be. They keep the quiet out; they do not
# pick the speaker, because loudness cannot — this board is half-duplex with no echo
# cancellation, and in the corpus a trigger the platform answered as noise reached
# 20395 RMS while a real sentence sat at 10875.
#BRIDGE_VAD_MIN_RMS=7500
#BRIDGE_VAD_FLOOR_RATIO=2.5

# How much silence after speech closes a turn, and how much speech a window must
# hold before it may close at all. The silence threshold is what the person waits
# out after their last word — it is spent on every turn by construction. The speech
# minimum is what keeps a door, a chair or the case being set down from being a turn,
# and 500 ms is measured, not chosen: over the 37 turns on record, every trigger the
# platform answered as noise needed 8 frames or fewer at 60 ms and every one it
# answered with a full sentence needed 9 or more. Below it a three-frame knock
# becomes a transcript, a reply and audio out of the gadget's speaker.
#BRIDGE_VAD_SILENCE_MS=900
#BRIDGE_VAD_MIN_SPEECH_MS=500

# The window speech is counted over. It must exceed BRIDGE_VAD_SILENCE_MS plus
# BRIDGE_VAD_MIN_SPEECH_MS, and the bridge refuses to start otherwise: speech that
# ages out of the window before the silence threshold is reached is a gadget that
# never answers at all, silently.
#BRIDGE_VAD_SPEECH_WINDOW_MS=3000

# The backstop — the longest a turn may run before it is closed anyway, for a person
# who never stops — and the other end, how long a window may hold no speech at all
# before it is given up on. The giveup is the line the log writes when a transient
# arrives in a room where nobody spoke.
#BRIDGE_MAX_TURN_MS=20000
#BRIDGE_VAD_NO_SPEECH_MS=15000

# How much audio before the first voiced frame is kept, so a soft onset is not
# clipped off the utterance; and how long after the bridge's own audio the device's
# microphone is distrusted, since the device reopens it before its speaker stops
# (D10). The guard is dropped when the platform produces a reply slower than
# realtime, where it would be spent before it was armed (D16).
#BRIDGE_ONSET_LEAD_MS=300
#BRIDGE_DRAIN_GUARD_MS=400

# How long a session may hold nobody before the bridge ends it, in ms. `0` turns the
# window off and holds a session open for as long as the device keeps it open — how
# this bridge behaved before the window existed, and the way back to that.
#
# Ending a session is what leaves the device Idle: microphone closed, wake word armed
# again, and the one state from which the firmware's own power management can run at
# all. A session held open is a gadget that never rests — and, in the other direction,
# a person who never needs the wake word. 60000 is the vendor server's own window,
# measured on 2026-10-10 at 60 s from the session hello, so adopting it changes nothing
# this deployment had not already been living with. See docs/vendor-server.md.
#BRIDGE_SESSION_IDLE_MS=60000

# Where this bridge's own music is: a directory of audio files it plays through the gadget's
# speaker when asked. Left unset there is no music at all, and that is not a degraded mode — the
# two commands ("phát nhạc", "dừng nhạc") go unrecognised and fall through as ordinary utterances,
# and no tool of the bridge's own is offered to a model, so a turn is asked for exactly as it was
# before any of this existed.
#
# Read when it is asked rather than at start, so a file dropped in while the bridge runs is
# playable, and a directory somebody deleted is "nothing to play" rather than a bridge that will
# not boot.
#BRIDGE_MUSIC_DIR=/srv/music

# How much quieter than the file a track is played, in dB. Negative plays it quieter, nought
# plays the file as it is — this is the one setting here that may be negative, because it is the
# difference between two levels rather than a level.
#
# Why there is a default at all. While the gadget is speaking, its own playback reaches its
# wake-word detector undiminished — the firmware turns voice processing off in that state — so
# whatever the bridge plays over its own voice leaves the person no opening to be heard. Measured
# on this deployment 2026-10-12, over three tracks of its own library and six synthesis draws: a
# track's RMS sits **10.4 dB** above the voice it speaks with, and a reply spends only 21-34% of
# its time near its own peak where a track spends 63-76%. Music is *continuous* as well as louder,
# which is why a wake word is heard over a reply and not over a track.
#
# SO SETTING NOTHING IS NOT "no change": music will play about 10 dB quieter than it did before
# this setting existed. That is the point of it — it is what leaves the opening — and a deployment
# that wants the previous level says so: 0 is a decision to leave it unmet, not a way to be left
# alone. -10 is the measured figure; whether it is enough for the wake word to be heard over the
# music is a question for the board, which is why this is a setting and not a constant.
#BRIDGE_MUSIC_GAIN_DB=-10

# TWO SETTINGS THAT USED TO BE HERE ARE GONE, and a bridge that still records
# either one refuses to start rather than ignoring it: the language and the engine
# are bound per device now, in BRIDGE_DEVICE_LANGUAGES and BRIDGE_DEVICE_ENGINES
# above, so every device can be heard and answered differently. A leftover
# BRIDGE_LANGUAGE or BRIDGE_TURN_ENGINE is the one kind of mistake that is
# invisible — the bridge would start, report itself healthy, and answer a
# Vietnamese gadget in English — so the line is an error until it is deleted.
